# Part 2: 64 pseudo-random odd 24-bit seeds (seeds.txt order), block exponents E_n by edge_lift.c, n = 1..56; predictions P4 to P6 and PU in the header
# ## Part 2 (written before the run): pooling across finite seeds
# Question: is the doubling density seed-independent, and if so does the pooled value sit at 3/7 or 1/2?
# Object: block exponents E_n of the edge code's prefix (period of x mod 2^(n+1)), by the kit's lifting rule (edge_lift.c), 64 pseudo-random odd 24-bit seeds, n <= 56; doublings counted for n = 13..56 (past the universal start and the rain plateaus).
#  P4: pooled density in [0.41, 0.45] (3/7 = 0.4286); counterfactual: at or above 0.48 (favours 1/2) or at or below 0.38.
#  P5: pooled conditional frequencies given the two previous bits, unforced states, each in [0.45, 0.55]; the forced state (two doublings) exactly 0.
#  P6: no seed has three consecutive doublings of its block exponent (the Case A argument is seed-free).
#  PU: the spread of per-seed densities: report the standard deviation across seeds; a spread much larger than binomial (about 0.075 for 44 samples) would say the rate is seed-dependent.
